Date: Thu, 29 Apr 1993 09:50:20 -0700 (PDT)
From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: RAND: Warning: Objectivism may be hazardous to your health 

Perry reasonably asks:

> Could someone say precisely what is wrong with objectivism, other than
> the crappy epsistemology? Don't include things like "Objectivists
> smoke" or "Ayn Rand was a jerk who thought that her opinions on music
> were objectively true" either, since neither smoking nor Ayn Rand's
> personal opinions on art have anything really to do with the core of
> objectivism.

Well, before we all eat each other, let me step in on this one.  I
should establish some credentials here:  I've been an admirer of Rand's
work and many of her ideas since I first read _The Fountainhead_ and
_AS_ in junior high school (I'm sure I've read AS at least half a dozen
times since then).  I pushed her stuff on my oldest son when he was 13
(he didn't take to the novels, but devoured all of her essays that he
could get his hands on in about two weeks, and has made himself very
unpopular with his schoolteachers ever since).  And I haven't had much
direct contact with Randroids, so I don't have any personal axe to
grind there.  But --

What's wrong with (at least some) Objectivists (the capital O is
important here) is that they *believe* the crappy epistemology.
They take very seriously the notion that not only is it possible
to develop objective, rigorously logical morality and esthetics,
but that Rand has done it.  This is stupid.  The worst of them
also take seriously her ideas about psychology, and believe that
by programming themselves with Objectivist rants they give themselves
a clear understanding of their own motivations, thought processes,
and actions.  This is terminally stupid.  It really is dangerous
to your health.  To top this all off, they then broadcast their
contempt for anyone who doesn't subscribe to the program--witness
the various Objectivist screeds about Libertarians, for example.

I assume that these Objectivists, and *their* Objectivism, are the
source of our objectivist-bashers' venom.  Obviously, Perry, you
(and I) don't exemplify this kind of stupidity.  Still, you call
yourself an objectivist, and therefore take the exception to their
rhetoric.  (You must realize, I'm sure, that many Objectivists would 
take strong exception to a relativist wimp like you having the gall 
to call yourself an objectivist).  Maybe there are people on the list
who are ready to consider any admirer of Rand to be a dangerous loon,
because some are.  If so, that is unfortunate; those folks are not
thinking clearly.  (Just as there may be those on the list who are ready to
consider any admirer of Korzybski, or Wilhelm Reich, or any self-professed
Neopagan, or whatever, to be an irrational loon, on the grounds that
there are indubitably many irrational loons in each of those categories.
Again this is a sign of muddy thinking).  But on the other hand, I
don't think you're participating cooperatively in the debate by
excluding the crappy epistemology from it--my understanding of the 
orthodox Objectivist view is that the epistemology is "the core of
objectivism", that the aspects of her thought that many of the
rest of us admire in spite of the crappy epistemology follow inevitably 
from the epistemology, and that just sharing some of Rand's conclusions does
not itself make you an objectivist unless you buy the whole package.
That version of Objectivism is objectively loony.

Scott DeLancey



