> premises, taking the responsibility to think, decide and act, and of course
> a strong advocacy of capitalism and individual rights.
 
Objectivism's weak spot is in metaphysics.  Indeed, it's the belief that there
*is* a `metaphysics' separable from epistemology that underlies its failures.

Correspondingly, Objectivism's emphasis on "reason" as a means of knowledge
is dangerously incomplete, because it tends to detach Objectivists from
dealing with reality into arguing about a maze of soi-disant "logical"
abstractions.  (This failure mode is typical of rationalist philosophies,
as opposed to empiricist ones).

It is worth noting that Objectivist ethics came first, in the '20s and '30s,
whan Rand was at the peak of her powers.  The epistemology was a late
development, and didn't actually stabilize until at least the late '50s.
 
> General Semantics (heretically cast in philosophical terms) [Korzybski]
> -----------------------------------------------------------------------
> 'Metaphysics'  - organically related space-time process-events, especially on
>                  quantum/atomic/molecular/colloidal levels.  A 'reality'
>                  that we can never directly 'perceive'.
> 'Epistemology' - scientific method, non-Aristotelian wholistic formulations,
>                  heavy emphasis on our neural natures and on the tentative
>                  nature of our 'knowledge'.
> 'Ethics'       - maximal effort with minimal expectation, seeking agreement
>                  and cooperation through rigorously accurate verbal maps.
> 'Politics'     - state capitalism --> international capitalism, controlled
>                  by a rational, scientific elite.
> 'Esthetics'    - ?
> 
>   Emphasis seems to be on making ever better maps of the territory, recording 
> and passing them on, practicing scientific method and a natural order of 
> abstracting (ie, observe --> describe --> evaluate/infer/generalize/etc.)
 
I'm not aware of any "state-capitalist" subtext in GS, nor of any political
subtext at all, for that matter.

GS has nothing explicit or prescriptive to say about esthetics.   However, I
see a natural link to "reader-response" criticism in literature, and certain
structuralist ideas in art criticism generally.

Allow me to suggest friendly amendments to the `Metaphysics' and `Epistemology'
sections:

`Metaphysics'  - We experience "reality", but we can only perceive, reason and
                 communicate about it through neural, cognitive and linguistic
                 filters.  Thus, metaphysics is not separable from epistemology.
`Epistemology' - Operationalism: what we know is what we can verify by
                 predictive test.  Empiricism: Facts beat theories.  Rejection
                 of rationalist claims of the primacy of logic.  Rigorous
                 separation between formal and predictive "truth".  Scientific
                 method, emphasis on the conditionality and tentativeness of
                 knowledge.
 
> Essentials of Extropian Philosophy [More]
> -----------------------------------------
> Metaphysics  - scientific reality with emphasis on (local) reversals
>                of entropy.
> Epistemology - scientific method, "hyper-rationality", intelligent technology.
> Ethics       - self-transformation, dynamic optimism, boundless expansion.
> Politics     - anarcho-capitalism, PPL, spontaneous order.
> Esthetics    - some science fiction (eg, Vinge, Heinlein)
> 
>   Emphasis seems to be on maximally extending one's life, autonomy, real 
> wealth, self-development, knowledge and information access.
 
I wouldn't call "intelligent technology" a component of epistemology in any
sense.
 
> Some comparisons/contrasts:
> ---------------------------
>   If my synopses are accurate, then each of these "philosophies" acknowledges
> the existence of an "objective" reality which can be known (to greater or
> lesser degrees) by the appropriate use of our minds.  They seem to diverge
> from there, however.  O and E seem ethically close (though non-identical) in 
> that they focus on the self, while GS seems more to focus on communality.

I question the assertion of a GS "focus on communality".  I don't think GS
implies any position on the individualist/communalist axis.  What is your
evidence for believing otherwise?

> O and E seem politically close (except for Randian denial of anarchy), while
> GS bombs out.  All three seem a little weak in Esthetics, although this
> observation may only be a function of my ignorance of the field.

Your belief that a complete philosophy must include a normative esthetics
is a cultural hangover from the ancient Greeks.  Forget it.

>   BTW, are there other relevant "philosophies" which should be considered 
> in this synthesis?

I'd be tempted to add:

Zen Buddhism (Bodhidharma, Mu-mon, Hakuin)
------------------------------------------
Metaphysics  - "Five pounds of flax!"  Metaphysics is a chew-toy for the
               distracted; experience is the only reality we can know
               outside of a few moments of enlightenment, and the reality
               of enlightenment is not communicable.
Epistemology - "The mind is like a dog.  When its master points at the moon,
               it barks at the hand." 
Ethics:        Honesty, simplicity, dedication to craft, total personal
               responsibility.  Detachment from desire, then detachment
               from detachment.
Politics:      Radical individualism.  Only enlightenment matters.
Esthetics:     Naturalness, roughness, incomplete statement, simplicity.
               Art mirrors the imperfection of the world and the evanescence
               of knowledge.

Have I ever mentioned here that I'm a student of Zen?   I see deep
connections between the Zen critique of "the mind as drunken monkey"
and General Semantics.
